sep 1.4.1 -> 1.4.1+ufwbpp.1: deterministic and linear-time source extraction
on Windows (MSVC builds), behaviour-neutral elsewhere.

Applied by scripts/build_sep_wheel.py to the hash-pinned sep==1.4.1 sdist
(sha256 a0c8324ab66ee716080472cb212e4303e6c3e33b0d43763075b20d4cab793b25).

Modified 2026-09-21 for Ultra-Fast WBPP; this file is the complete set of
changes against upstream sep 1.4.1 (LGPL-3.0-or-later, see licenses/), kept
in the repository so the modified library remains rebuildable from source:

1. src/sepcore.h, src/deblend.c: MSVC has no rand_r(); upstream maps it to
   rand(), whose per-thread state the `randseed = 1` reset in sep_extract()
   never touches.  Every extraction in a thread therefore continued the
   previous random sequence and gatherup() assigned faint deblended pixels
   differently from call to call (object counts and fluxes varied on
   identical input).  sep_rand_r() advances the caller's seed like POSIX
   rand_r(), returning 0..32767 to match this platform's RAND_MAX.

2. src/lutz.c, src/deblend.c: the Lutz scan buffers (info, store, marker,
   psstack, start, end) and the deblending tables (son, ok, objlist) are
   allocated zero-initialised, and the son table's tail is cleared after it
   grows, so heap contents can never reach a deblending decision.  This
   matches what upstream already does for the main-scan buffers in
   extract.c.

3. src/extract.h, src/extract.c, src/lutz.c, src/deblend.c: addobjdeep()
   grows the object and pixel arrays geometrically instead of by one object
   per call.  The exact-size realloc copied the whole list for every
   detection, which is quadratic on allocators that cannot extend a block in
   place (the Windows heap) and made extraction cost per object grow with
   the object count.  Logical sizes and outputs are unchanged.

4. PKG-INFO: the sdist version becomes 1.4.1+ufwbpp.1 so that
   sep.__version__ and importlib.metadata identify the patched build.

Outputs (all catalog columns and the segmentation map) are bit-identical to
the upstream wheel on macOS for crowded synthetic fields with deblending.

